# The Lindenbaum algebra of variable-free formulas

A formula is variable-free when it contains no propositional variable; write
$`\mathrm{Fm}_0` for the set of variable-free formulas.

{stmt}`atomFree`

{docstring atomFree +allowMissing}

{srcLink}`atomFree`

$`F_0` is the quotient of $`\mathrm{Fm}_0` by $`\dashv\vdash`, ordered by
$`\vdash`.  Since every connective respects $`\dashv\vdash`, it is a Heyting
algebra with meet $`\land`, join $`\lor`, implication $`\twoheadrightarrow`,
bottom $`\bot` and top $`\top`, and by the previous section $`\bigcirc` is a
nucleus on it.  Every element is the value of a term built from $`\bot` alone,
so $`F_0` is generated by its bottom element; by the usual Lindenbaum argument,
which is not mechanised here, it is the initial Heyting algebra with a
nucleus: for every Heyting algebra $`H` with a nucleus $`j` there is exactly one
homomorphism $`F_0 \to H` preserving the Heyting operations and sending
$`\bigcirc` to $`j`.  That $`F_0` is infinite is PROVED.

