{docstring rn +allowMissing}

{srcLink}`rn`

Substituting $`\bigcirc\bot` for $`p` gives variable-free formulas
$`t_n`; the first six are

$$`t_0 = \bot,\quad t_1 = \bigcirc\bot,\quad t_2 = \lnot\bigcirc\bot,\quad t_3 = t_1 \lor t_2,\quad t_4 = t_3 \twoheadrightarrow t_1 \;(\dashv\vdash \lnot\lnot\bigcirc\bot),\quad t_5 = t_3 \lor t_4.`

{stmt}`rnSub`

{docstring rnSub +allowMissing}

{srcLink}`rnSub`

The order among the $`t_n` is read off one Kripke frame, the _ladder_
$`\mathcal{L}`: its worlds are $`\mathbb{N}`, a world $`x` sees $`y` (written
$`x \preceq y`) when $`y = x` or $`y + 2 \le x`, and $`p` holds at $`0` only.  On
it the odd rungs have the initial segments $`[0, k]` as truth sets and the even
rungs $`[0, k-1] \cup \{k+1\}`:

